    <Program class="ca.nrcan.gc.OEE.ERS.ErsProgram">
        <Labels>
            <English>EnerGuide Rating System</English>
            <French>SystÃ¨me de cote ÃnerGuide</French>
        </Labels>
        <Version major="15" minor="4" build="5">
            <Labels>
                <English>v15.4b5</English>
                <French>v15.4b5</French>
            </Labels>
        </Version>
        <SdkVersion major="1" minor="16">
            <Labels>
                <English>v1.16</English>
                <French>v1.16</French>
            </Labels>
        </SdkVersion>
        <Options>
            <Main applyHouseholdOperatingConditions="true" applyReducedOperatingConditions="false" atypicalElectricalLoads="false" waterConservation="true" referenceHouse="false">
                <Vermiculite code="3">
                    <English>No Vermiculite</English>
                    <French>Pas de Vermiculite</French>
                </Vermiculite>
            </Main>
            <HouseholdOperatingConditions numberOfOccupants="4" coolingSeasonMonths="4" isClothesWasherEnergySaver="true" isLightingEnergySaver="true" areAppliancesEnergySaver="true">
                <Thermostat heatingDaytime="20.5" heatingNighttime="21.5" cooling="25" />
            </HouseholdOperatingConditions>
            <WaterConservation LowFlushToilets="0" LowFlushToiletsUpgrade="2" />
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;

Overall, your house is already quite air tight, with an air change rate of approximately 1.5 air changes per hour at 50Pa.  I did note some air leakage around your doors, and at the connection between your walls and ceiling, particularly over your kitchen cupboards. 



I do not recommend undertaking any air sealing work on your home at this time. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially &lt;b&gt;spend an additional $25&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, increase your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;0.7 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a home energy evaluation, which includes an air leakage test, before and after your retrofits. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x6 studs with 6 inches (R20) of fiberglass batt insulation. I have modelled adding 3 inches (R15) of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation to bring your total insulation value up to approximately R32.



Adding insulation to the exterior of your walls is typically done at the same time as a siding replacement. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding R10 of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $870&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;22.9 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$1,875 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 0.7 (R-3.8). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</mainWalls>
                <foundation>

Your crawlspace walls are 2x4 framing with no insulation. I have modelled adding 4 inches (R12) fibreglass batt insulation to the interior of your crawlspace walls.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding 4 inches (R12) fibreglass batt insulation to the crawlspace walls, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $80&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;2.2 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate 100 percent of the crawl space wall area, including the header area. The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</foundation>
                <windows>

As all of your windows are older triple pane ones that do not appear to have a Low E coating or an insulating gas fill between the panes of glass, I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading all of them with ENERGY STARÂ® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing all of your windows with triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged, ENERGY STARÂ® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $155&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;4.1 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</windows>
                <VentilationSystem>

Your home is already quite airtight, even before undertaking any upgrades. Proper ventilation protects a home from unwanted toxins, pollutants, and odors and can also help protect the overall structure of a home or building by eliminating excess moisture from the air. An energy-efficient heat recovery ventilator is one of the best ways to control indoor air quality.



I recommend that you have an ENERGY STARÂ® rated Heat Recovery Ventilator (HRV) system designed and installed by an individual who holds current certification from an organization, such as HRAI. 



Adding an HRV will increase your annual energy costs, but will improve the air quality in your home and help maintain a healthy indoor environment. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By installing an ENERGY STARÂ® rated HRV, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;spend an additional $160&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, and increase your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;3.4 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing an HRV from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;</VentilationSystem>
                <HotWaterSystem />
                <HeatingSystem>

Your current propane fired furnace was originally rated at 90% AFUE. When it is time to replace it, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. If you choose a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace, the current chimney can be eliminated altogether.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your heating system with a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $205&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;5.6 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables>

Harnessing solar energy in the NWT may seem unlikely. The short and dark days of winter provide little or no usable energy, depending on the latitude in the NWT. However, the long hours of sunlight in spring and summer can provide usable heat and electricity for up to eight months of the year.  As prices continue to drop, solar PV panels are becoming competitive with the cost of diesel generation. 



For the purposes of this report, I have modelled a 3 kW system. Depending on your roof, you may be able to install a larger system, or you may need to install a smaller system. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By installing a 3 kW solar photovoltaic system, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $785&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;8.9 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$5,000 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program. 



&lt;b&gt; GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program rebate amount differs according to the purchase cost of the system. You would be eligible for 1/3 of the purchase cost, up to a maximum of $5,000 per year. Applicants are strongly encouraged to contact the AEA for pre-approval prior to installation. Projects will be funded on a first come, first serve basis until the program is fully subscribed. Projects must be installed and operational to receive the rebate. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/alternative-energy-technologies-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</Renewables>
                <WaterConservation>

I recommend you replace both your toilets. When replacing your toilets, purchase 4.8 Liters or less, or dual-flush toilets. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing your toilet(s) with a new 4.8 Liters or less, or dual-flush toilet(s), you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save ~$50 &lt;/b&gt; per person, per year in water and energy savings.  There are currently no rebates available for toilets from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</WaterConservation>
                <AdditionalComments>

&lt;b&gt;ECM Pumps:&lt;/b&gt;

Electronically Commutated Motors (ECM) save pumping energy for circulation pumps and hydronic heating systems, such as baseboard convectors and in floor radiant by at least 50% and usually much more compared with other pumps. Typical pumps use a constant amount of power regardless of how much water needs to be circulated throughout the building, and are often significantly oversized. By contrast an ECM pump senses how hard it has to work to circulate the heating fluid and adjusts the flow rate accordingly. In addition, by reducing the flow, they can also increase the temperature difference between water leaving the boiler and the water returning which also saves heating fuel by increasing boiler efficiency.  These pumps determine the minimal output required to achieve optimal comfort. 



Some ECM circulators use an integrated logic board to ÂlearnÂ the systemÂs usage patterns in order to boost the standard 50 percent energy savings into the 70-80 percent range. As the valves in the different sections open or close with rising or falling demand, the pumps automatically ramp up or down to meet the load. Even though these pumps are typically 2-3 times more expensive, with the huge savings potential, their payback is typically very quick.  When you purchase and install an ECM pump in your house you can qualify for &lt;b&gt;$175 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



&lt;Additional Comments:&lt;/b&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Fuel Costs - Inuvik&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Inuvik:

Electricity - $18.90 min, $0.31 per kWh

Natural gas - $37.21 per GJ

Oil  - $1.21 per litre 

Propane - $1.52 per litre

Wood - $472.50 per cord 

Pellets - $11.25 per bag



